    "case_summary": "A 43-year-old woman with a history of remote alcohol use disorder presented with worsening epigastric pain radiating to the back, associated with nausea, vomiting, and early satiety. She did not report a family history of autoimmune, gastric, or pancreaticobiliary disease.\n\nPatient was later found to be tachycardic and tachypneic with an oxygen requirement of 4 L.\n\n- Laboratory tests:    Initial presentation showed:    - WBC: 20.8 x 10³/uL    - Elevated lipase: 273 U/L    - Total bilirubin: 2.5 mg/dL    - ALP: 200 IU/L    - AST: 156 IU/L    - ALT: 59 IU/L    - Triglycerides: 308 mg/dL        - Fluid Analysis:    - Ascitic fluid positive for amylase >1000 U/L    - Pleural fluid: transudative with positive amylase and lipase    - Imaging Studies:    1. CT abdomen and pelvis findings:       - Acute interstitial pancreatitis       - 6 × 7 cm pseudocyst causing gastric outlet obstruction       - Ascites without cirrhosis        2. Initial MRCP findings:       - Negative for leak       - No evidence of intraductal stones        3. ERCP findings:       - Neck, body, and tail of pancreatic duct did not opacify       - Later showed leakage into peritoneal cavity       - Fistula extending toward diaphragm        4. Chest radiograph:       - Large, right-sided pleural effusion        5. EUS findings:       - Demonstrated pancreatic duct anomaly",
